WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.074
An MCP architecture usually starts with a host application.

2
00:00:04.074 --> 00:00:10.145
The host is the AI application or work environment where the user interacts with the model.

3
00:00:10.145 --> 00:00:15.897
Inside or alongside the host, an MCP client communicates with one or more MCP servers.

4
00:00:15.897 --> 00:00:20.690
The server exposes capabilities such as tools, resources, or prompts.

5
00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:32.992
Those capabilities may connect to downstream systems such as local files, cloud services, developer platforms, ticketing systems, databases, monitoring tools, or internal APIs.

6
00:00:32.992 --> 00:00:38.663
The host, client, and server roles matter because they separate responsibilities.

7
00:00:38.663 --> 00:00:44.735
The host decides how the user experience works and how model interaction is orchestrated.

8
00:00:44.735 --> 00:00:48.090
The client handles the MCP connection to a server.

9
00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:55.918
The server describes available capabilities and carries out requests according to its implementation and policy.

10
00:00:55.918 --> 00:01:04.705
This separation helps an AI application use external systems without embedding every integration detail directly into the host.

11
00:01:04.705 --> 00:01:07.022
MCP servers can be local or remote.

12
00:01:07.022 --> 00:01:12.614
A local server may run on the same machine as the host and expose local tools or files.

13
00:01:12.614 --> 00:01:21.241
A remote server may run as a network service and connect to a cloud platform, SaaS product, enterprise API, or shared data source.

14
00:01:21.241 --> 00:01:25.635
Local and remote models create different operational concerns.

15
00:01:25.635 --> 00:01:30.348
Local servers may raise workstation access and execution questions.

16
00:01:30.348 --> 00:01:38.656
Remote servers may raise authentication, transport, tenant isolation, availability, and vendor governance questions.

17
00:01:38.656 --> 00:01:42.650
Identity flows through this architecture in several ways.

18
00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:46.724
A user identity may represent the person making the request.

19
00:01:46.724 --> 00:01:49.999
An application identity may represent the host.

20
00:01:49.999 --> 00:01:54.872
A service identity may represent the server or downstream integration.

21
00:01:54.872 --> 00:02:00.304
The model may propose a tool call, but it should not become the permission system.

22
00:02:00.304 --> 00:02:12.446
MCP helps separate AI application behavior from external system integration, but teams still need to design authorization, consent, logging, and ownership around the full path.
